{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073445 :question/math-academy-id "e-13009" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y &=4 \\\\ x+2y &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $y$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =4 \\\\ x & =4+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we replace $x$ with $4+y$ in the second equation and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tx+2y &=16 \\\\\n\t(4+y) + 2y &=16\\\\\n\t4+3y &=16\\\\\n\t3y &=12\\\\\n\ty &=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into $x=4+y$, then we can find the value of $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\tx &= 4 + y \\\\\n\t   &= 4 + (4) \\\\\n\t   &=8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x = 8$ and $y = 4$, which can be written as $(8, 4).$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074030 :question/math-academy-id "q-171690" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x + 5y &=13 \\\\ y &=x + 5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\nThe solution is\n$($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074172 :question/math-academy-id "q-4133" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3a + 2b &=4 \\\\ - 4a + b &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074185 :question/math-academy-id "q-5179" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p-q?$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p &=15 \\\\ p+2q &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $p$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p & =15 \\\\ p & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $p=5$ into $p+2q=9,$ then we can find the value of $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+2q & =9 \\\\ (5)+2q & =9 \\\\ 2q & =4 \\\\ q & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=5,\\,q=2.$ So,\n\n$$\np-q=5-2=3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233560 :question/math-academy-id "q-167194" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $20b+400a=7\\,200.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=480-10a \\\\ 20b+400a &=7\\,200\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=480-10a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20b+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 20(480-10a)+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600-200a+400a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 9\\,600+200a & =7\\,200 \\\\ 200a & =-2\\,400 \\\\ a & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-12$ into $b=480-10a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =480-10(-12) \\\\ b & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-12,\\,b=600.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233567 :question/math-academy-id "q-167210" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $30b+900a=6\\,000.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=240-20a \\\\ 30b+900a &=6\\,000\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=240-20a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30b+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 30(240-20a)+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200-600a+900a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 7\\,200+300a & =6\\,000 \\\\ 300a & =-1\\,200 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-4$ into $b=240-20a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =240-20(-4) \\\\ b & =320\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=320.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233574 :question/math-academy-id "q-167226" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $70a+9b=600.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=75-5a \\\\ 70a+9b &=600\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=75-5a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70a+9b & =600 \\\\ 70a+9(75-5a) & =600 \\\\ 70a+675-45a & =600 \\\\ 25a+675 & =600 \\\\ 25a & =-75 \\\\ a & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-3$ into $b=75-5a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =75-5(-3) \\\\ b & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-3,\\,b=90.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496226 :question/math-academy-id "q-181149" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4u-6v &=-3 \\\\ u &=-v-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(u,v)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(u,v)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the second equation is already solved for $u:$\n\n$$\nu=-v-2\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $u$ with $-v-2$ in the first equation and solve for $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-6v & =-3 \\\\ 4(-v-2)-6v & =-3 \\\\ -4v-8-6v & =-3 \\\\ -10v & =5 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into $u=-v-2,$ then we can find the value of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-v-2 \\\\ u & =-(-\\frac{1}{2})-2 \\\\ u & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=-\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496255 :question/math-academy-id "q-181175" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y &=-4 \\\\ x-3y &=-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(x,y)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the second equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $x$ by adding $3y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\nx=3y-8\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $x$ with $3y-8$ in the first equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =-4 \\\\ -2(3y-8)+y & =-4 \\\\ -6y+16+y & =-4 \\\\ -5y & =-20 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $y=4$ into $x=3y-8,$ then we can find the value of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3y-8 \\\\ x & =3(4)-8 \\\\ x & =12-8 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=4,\\,y=4,$ which can be expressed as $(4,4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497411 :question/math-academy-id "q-166843" :question/problem "The pastry shop's total cost and revenue per month (in dollars) from selling cakes can be modeled by the equations $y=1\\,600+5n$ and $y=64+17n,$ respectively, where $n$ is the number of cakes sold. \n\nSolve the system below using the substitution method to find the exact number of cakes needed to break even and the revenue made.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1\\,600+5n \\\\ y &=64+17n\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe pastry shop breaks even when$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes are produced. \n\nThe revenue made when selling this number of cakes is$\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$dollars." :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that $y$ is isolated in the first equation. Therefore, we can replace $y$ with $1\\,600+5n$ in the second equation and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =64+17n \\\\ 1\\,600+5n & =64+17n \\\\ 1\\,600+5n-64 & =64+17n-64 \\\\ 1\\,536+5n & =17n \\\\ 1\\,536+5n-5n & =17n-5n \\\\ 1\\,536 & =12n \\\\ \\frac{1\\,536}{12} & =\\frac{12n}{12} \\\\ 128 & =n \\\\ n & =128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $n=128$ into $y=64+17n$, then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=64+17(128)=2\\,240\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solution to the system of equations is $n=128$ and $y=2\\,240.$\nTherefore, the pastry shop breaks even when $128$ units are produced, and the revenue made when selling this number of units is $2\\,240$ dollars."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503753 :question/math-academy-id "q-166761" :question/problem "Mary wonders where and when they'd meet if Sam left home $3.8$ minutes earlier and walked with a speed of $50$ meters per minute. She finds that the distance $d$ from home they'd meet can be modeled using the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}d+60t &=960 \\\\ d-50t &=190.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time (in minutes) since Mary left the community park.\n\nMary and Sam would meet$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$minutes after Mary left the community park.\n\nMary and Sam would meet at a distance of $\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,$meters from home." :question/worked-solution "To solve the given system of linear equations, we will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the second equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $d$ adding to both sides of the equation $50t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d-50t &=190 \\\\ d &=190+50t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $d$ with $190+50t$ in the first equation and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d+60t=960 \\\\ (190+50t)+60t & =960 \\\\ 190+110t & =960 \\\\ 110t & =960-190 \\\\ 110t & =770 \\\\ t & =\\frac{770}{110} \\\\ t & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, they would have met after $t=7$ minutes.\n\nIf we substitute $t=7$ into $d=190+50t$, we can find the value of $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =190+50t \\\\  & =190+50(7) \\\\  & =540\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, they meet at a distance of $540$ meters from home."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508171 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$\\{3a-3b=-18; -2a+b=7$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2a+b & =7 \\\\ b & =7+2a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=7+2a$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-3b & =-18 \\\\ 3a-3(7+2a) & =-18 \\\\ 3a-21-6a & =-18 \\\\ -3a-21 & =-18 \\\\ -3a & =3 \\\\ a & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-1$ into $b=7+2a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =7+2(-1) \\\\ b & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a-b=-1-(5)=-6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508174 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n$\\{x+5y=21; y=x-3$\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.The solution is\n$($\n{{field-1}}\n$, $\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the second equation is already solved for $y:$\n\n$$\ny=x-3\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $y$ in the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5y & =21 \\\\ x+5(x-3) & =21 \\\\ x+5x-15 & =21 \\\\ 6x-15 & =21 \\\\ 6x & =36 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=6$ into $y=x-3,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(6)-3 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=6,\\,y=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508179 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$\\{-3a-2b=2; -3a+b=8$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3a+b & =8 \\\\ b & =8+3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=8+3a$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3a-2b & =2 \\\\ -3a-2(8+3a) & =2 \\\\ -3a-16-6a & =2 \\\\ -9a-16 & =2 \\\\ -9a & =18 \\\\ a & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-2$ into $b=8+3a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =8+3(-2) \\\\ b & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a-b=-2-(2)=-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508187 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$\\{3x+3y=2; 3x+y=-5$\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $, $ {{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =-5 \\\\ y & =-5-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-5-3x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3y & =2 \\\\ 3x+3(-5-3x) & =2 \\\\ 3x-15-9x & =2 \\\\ -6x-15 & =2 \\\\ -6x & =17 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=-\\frac{17}{6}$ into $y=-5-3x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5-3(-\\frac{17}{6}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-\\frac{17}{6},\\,y=\\frac{7}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508204 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$\\{-3x+4y=8; -2x+y=6$\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $, $ {{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ y & =6+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=6+2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y & =8 \\\\ -3x+4(6+2x) & =8 \\\\ -3x+24+8x & =8 \\\\ 5x+24 & =8 \\\\ 5x & =-16 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=-\\frac{16}{5}$ into $y=6+2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6+2(-\\frac{16}{5}) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-\\frac{16}{5},\\,y=-\\frac{2}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508205 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$\\{-5a+2b=9; -3a+b=3$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3a+b & =3 \\\\ b & =3+3a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=3+3a$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5a+2b & =9 \\\\ -5a+2(3+3a) & =9 \\\\ -5a+6+6a & =9 \\\\ a+6 & =9 \\\\ a & =3 \\\\ a & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=3$ into $b=3+3a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =3+3(3) \\\\ b & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a-b=3-(12)=-9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508207 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p - q$?\n\n$\\{4p=-24; p+5q=19$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $p$ by dividing both sides by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p & =-24 \\\\ p & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $p=-6$ into the second equation and solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p+5q & =19 \\\\ (-6)+5q & =19 \\\\ 5q & =25 \\\\ q & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p-q=-6-(5)=-11.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508210 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$\\{-3a-3b=6; -4a+b=-7$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4a+b & =-7 \\\\ b & =-7+4a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=-7+4a$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3a-3b & =6 \\\\ -3a-3(-7+4a) & =6 \\\\ -3a+21-12a & =6 \\\\ -15a+21 & =6 \\\\ -15a & =-15 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=1$ into $b=-7+4a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =-7+4(1) \\\\ b & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a-b=1-(-3)=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508214 :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ satisfy $b=135-11a.$\n\nThe coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $7b+162a=435.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=135-11a \\\\ 7b+162a &=435\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=135-11a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7b+162a & =435 \\\\ 7(135-11a)+162a & =435 \\\\ 945-77a+162a & =435 \\\\ 945+85a & =435 \\\\ 85a & =-510 \\\\ a & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-6$ into $b=135-11a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =135-11(-6) \\\\ b & =201\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-6,\\,b=201.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508215 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$\\{-2a-4b=-14; -2a+b=6$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2a+b & =6 \\\\ b & =6+2a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=6+2a$ into the first equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2a-4b & =-14 \\\\ -2a-4(6+2a) & =-14 \\\\ -2a-24-8a & =-14 \\\\ -10a-24 & =-14 \\\\ -10a & =10 \\\\ a & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-1$ into $b=6+2a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =6+2(-1) \\\\ b & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a-b=-1-(4)=-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508227 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$\\{5x-2y=5; 2x+y=-1$\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $, $ {{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-1-2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-2y & =5 \\\\ 5x-2(-1-2x) & =5 \\\\ 5x+2+4x & =5 \\\\ 9x+2 & =5 \\\\ 9x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into $y=-1-2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1-2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=-\\frac{5}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508233 :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$\\{2x+2y=12; -2x+y=5$\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $, $ {{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =5 \\\\ y & =5+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=5+2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =12 \\\\ 2x+2(5+2x) & =12 \\\\ 2x+10+4x & =12 \\\\ 6x+10 & =12 \\\\ 6x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into $y=5+2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=\\frac{17}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546971 :question/math-academy-id "q-52115" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a-b?$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a &=7 \\\\ 3a+6b &=15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It so happens that the first equation is already solved for $a:$\n\n$$\na=7\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=7$ into $3a+6b=15,$ then we can find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a+6b & =15 \\\\ 3(7)+6b & =15 \\\\ 6b & =-6 \\\\ b & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=7,\\,b=-1.$ So,\n\n$$\na-b=7-(-1)=7+1=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558573 :question/math-academy-id "q-181154" :question/problem "Consider the following system  of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=3 \\\\ x-y &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(x,y)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $x$ by dividing both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ into $x-y=1,$ then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}-y & =1 \\\\ -y & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{3}{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186562396 :question/math-academy-id "q-171693" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-u+v &=-1 \\\\ 3u+3v &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(u,v)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(u,v)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. It doesn't matter which equation we pick first, so we choose the easiest one. In this case, the first equation looks like the easiest.\n\nUsing the first equation, we can isolate $v$ by adding $u$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\nv=u-1\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $v$ with $u-1$ in the second equation and solve for $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3u+3v & =5 \\\\ 3u+3(u-1) & =5 \\\\ 3u+3u-3 & =5 \\\\ 6u & =8 \\\\ u & =\\frac{8}{6} \\\\ u & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $u=\\frac{4}{3}$ into $v=u-1,$ then we can find the value of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u-1 \\\\ v & =\\frac{4}{3}-1 \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=\\frac{4}{3},\\,v=\\frac{1}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{4}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563725 :question/math-academy-id "q-167218" :question/problem "The coefficients $a$ and $b$ also satisfy the equation $100a+15b=4\\,800.$ Find the values of $a$ and $b.$\n\n$a\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$b\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}b &=270-15a \\\\ 100a+15b &=4\\,800\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=270-15a\n$$\n\nSo, we replace $b$ in the second equation and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100a+15b & =4\\,800 \\\\ 100a+15(270-15a) & =4\\,800 \\\\ 100a+4\\,050-225a & =4\\,800 \\\\ -125a+4\\,050 & =4\\,800 \\\\ -125a & =750 \\\\ a & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $a=-6$ into $b=270-15a,$ we can then find the value of $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =270-15(-6) \\\\ b & =360\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-6,\\,b=360.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073753 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073446 :question/math-academy-id "e-13010" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2u+9v-6 &=0 \\\\ 2u+6v-8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n\n$$\n\\qquad\\:\\:\\: \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\color{red}2u+9v-6} &{\\color{red}{}=0} && {\\color{red}(1)} \\\\ {\\color{blue}2u+6v-8} &{\\color{blue}{}=0} && {\\color{blue}(2)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+6v-8 &= 0 \\\\\n2u+6v &= 8 \\\\\n2u &= 8 - 6v \\\\\nu &= \\dfrac{8-6v}{2} \\\\\nu &= 4 - 3v\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-3v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2(4-3v)+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t8-6v+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n2+3v &= 0 \\\\\n3v &= -2 \\\\\nv &= -\\dfrac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t2u+9v-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u+9\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right) -6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-6-6 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u-12 &= 0 \\\\\n\t2u &= 12 \\\\\n\tu &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=6$ and $v=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$, which can be written as $\\left(6,-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right).$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073997 :question/math-academy-id "q-12444" :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3p + q &=-9 \\\\ 5p + 4q &=32\\end{aligned}\\right.$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073998 :question/math-academy-id "q-12457" :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7a + 2b &=18 \\\\ -6a + 6b &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074047 :question/math-academy-id "q-18483" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p - q$?\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5q + 55 &=0 \\\\ 2p + 3q + 3 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496252 :question/math-academy-id "q-112214" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $y-x?$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}21x-9y &=0 \\\\ 5y-15x+10 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}21x-9y &=0 && \\,\\,(1) \\\\ 5y-15x+10 &=0 && \\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(2),$ we can isolate $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-15x+10 & =0 \\\\ 5y & =15x-10 \\\\ y & =3x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $3x-2$  into equation $(1)$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21x-9y & =0 \\\\ 21x-9(3x-2) & =0 \\\\ 21x-27x+18 & =0 \\\\ -6x+18 & =0 \\\\ 18 & =6x \\\\ 3 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=3$ into $y=3x-2,$ then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-2 \\\\ y & =3(3)-2 \\\\ y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=3,\\,y=7.$ So,\n\n$$\ny-x=7-3=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496314 :question/math-academy-id "q-181207" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p-6q &=-18 \\\\ 4p+3q+2 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(p,q)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$(p,q)=($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3p-6q &=-18 && \\,\\,(1) \\\\ 4p+3q+2 &=0 && \\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(1),$ we can solve for $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-6q & =-18 \\\\ 3p & =6q-18 \\\\ p & =2q-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $p=2q-6$ into equation $(2)$ and solve for $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2q-6)+3q+2 & =0 \\\\ 8q-24+3q+2 & =0 \\\\ 11q & =22 \\\\ q & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $q=2$ into equation $(1),$ then we can find the value of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-6(2) & =-18 \\\\ 3p-12 & =-18 \\\\ 3p & =-6 \\\\ p & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-2,\\,q=2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505001 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{6x - 8y - 31 = 0; 2x - 4y - 6 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{6x - 8y - 31 = 0(1); 2x - 4y - 6 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x - 4y - 6 &= 0 \\\\ 2x - 4y &= 6 \\\\ 2x &= 6 + 4y \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{6 + 4y}{2} \\\\ x &= 3 + 2y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=3+2y$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6x - 8y - 31 &= 0 \\\\ 6(3 + 2y) - 8y - 31 &= 0 \\\\ 18 + 12y - 8y - 31 &= 0 \\\\ -13 + 4y &= 0 \\\\ 4y &= 13 \\\\ y &= \\dfrac{13}{4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{13}{4}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6x - 8y - 31 &= 0 \\\\ 6x - 8\\left(\\dfrac{13}{4}\\right) - 31 &= 0 \\\\ 6x - 26 - 31 &= 0 \\\\ 6x - 57 &= 0 \\\\ 6x &= 57 \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{19}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\dfrac{19}{2}$ and\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{13}{4}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{19}{2},\\dfrac{13}{4}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505002 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{7a - 8b + 41 = 0; 4a - 4b + 20 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{7a - 8b + 41 = 0(1); 4a - 4b + 20 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $a$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4a - 4b + 20 &= 0 \\\\ 4a - 4b &= -20 \\\\ 4a &= -20 + 4b \\\\ a &= \\dfrac{-20 + 4b}{4} \\\\ a &= -5 + b \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $a=-5+b$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7a - 8b + 41 &= 0 \\\\ 7(-5 + b) - 8b + 41 &= 0 \\\\ -35 + 7b - 8b + 41 &= 0 \\\\ 6 - b &= 0 \\\\ -b &= -6 \\\\ b &= 6 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nb=6\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7a - 8b + 41 &= 0 \\\\ 7a - 8\\left(6\\right) + 41 &= 0 \\\\ 7a - 48 + 41 &= 0 \\\\ 7a - 7 &= 0 \\\\ 7a &= 7 \\\\ a &= 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=1$ and\n\n$$\nb=6\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(1,6\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505004 :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n$\\{2x + y = -7; 2x - 3y = 13$." :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{2x + y + 7 = 0(1); 2x - 3y - 13 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(1)$, we can solve for $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y + 2x + 7 &= 0 \\\\ y + 2x &= -7 \\\\ y &= -7 - 2x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-7-2x$ into equation $\\color{red}(2)$ and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -3y + 2x - 13 &= 0 \\\\ -3(-7 - 2x) + 2x - 13 &= 0 \\\\ 21 + 6x + 2x - 13 &= 0 \\\\ 8 + 8x &= 0 \\\\ 8x &= -8 \\\\ x &= -1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nx=-1\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(2)$, then we can find the value of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -3y + 2x - 13 &= 0 \\\\ -3y + 2\\left(-1\\right) - 13 &= 0 \\\\ -3y - 2 - 13 &= 0 \\\\ -3y - 15 &= 0 \\\\ -3y &= 15 \\\\ y &= -5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $y=-5$ and\n\n$$\nx=-1\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(-1,-5\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505005 :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n\n$\\{-6p - 9q = -6; 3p + 3q = 6$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{-6p - 9q + 6 = 0(1); 3p + 3q - 6 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $q$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3q + 3p - 6 &= 0 \\\\ 3q + 3p &= 6 \\\\ 3q &= 6 - 3p \\\\ q &= \\dfrac{6 - 3p}{3} \\\\ q &= 2 - p \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $q=2-p$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -9q - 6p + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -9(2 - p) - 6p + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -18 + 9p - 6p + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -12 + 3p &= 0 \\\\ 3p &= 12 \\\\ p &= 4 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\np=4\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -9q - 6p + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -9q - 6\\left(4\\right) + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -9q - 24 + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -9q - 18 &= 0 \\\\ -9q &= 18 \\\\ q &= -2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $q=-2$ and\n\n$$\np=4\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(4,-2\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505044 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{6p - 4q + 68 = 0; 4p + 4q + 12 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{6p - 4q + 68 = 0(1); 4p + 4q + 12 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4p + 4q + 12 &= 0 \\\\ 4p + 4q &= -12 \\\\ 4p &= -12 - 4q \\\\ p &= \\dfrac{-12 - 4q}{4} \\\\ p &= -3 - q \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $p=-3-q$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6p - 4q + 68 &= 0 \\\\ 6(-3 - q) - 4q + 68 &= 0 \\\\ -18 - 6q - 4q + 68 &= 0 \\\\ 50 - 10q &= 0 \\\\ -10q &= -50 \\\\ q &= 5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nq=5\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 6p - 4q + 68 &= 0 \\\\ 6p - 4\\left(5\\right) + 68 &= 0 \\\\ 6p - 20 + 68 &= 0 \\\\ 6p + 48 &= 0 \\\\ 6p &= -48 \\\\ p &= -8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=-8$ and\n\n$$\nq=5\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(-8,5\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505050 :question/problem "Using the method of substitution, find the solution of\n\n$\\{5x + 2y = 17; 3x + 3y = 6$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{5x + 2y - 17 = 0(1); 3x + 3y - 6 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3y + 3x - 6 &= 0 \\\\ 3y + 3x &= 6 \\\\ 3y &= 6 - 3x \\\\ y &= \\dfrac{6 - 3x}{3} \\\\ y &= 2 - x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=2-x$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2y + 5x - 17 &= 0 \\\\ 2(2 - x) + 5x - 17 &= 0 \\\\ 4 - 2x + 5x - 17 &= 0 \\\\ -13 + 3x &= 0 \\\\ 3x &= 13 \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{13}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{13}{3}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2y + 5x - 17 &= 0 \\\\ 2y + 5\\left(\\dfrac{13}{3}\\right) - 17 &= 0 \\\\ 2y + \\dfrac{65}{3} - 17 &= 0 \\\\ 2y + \\dfrac{14}{3} &= 0 \\\\ 2y &= -\\dfrac{14}{3} \\\\ y &= -\\dfrac{7}{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $y=-\\dfrac{7}{3}$ and\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{13}{3}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{13}{3},-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505053 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-6p + 4q + 14 = 0; 2p + 4q + 6 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{-6p + 4q + 14 = 0(1); 2p + 4q + 6 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2p + 4q + 6 &= 0 \\\\ 2p + 4q &= -6 \\\\ 2p &= -6 - 4q \\\\ p &= \\dfrac{-6 - 4q}{2} \\\\ p &= -3 - 2q \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $p=-3-2q$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -6p + 4q + 14 &= 0 \\\\ -6(-3 - 2q) + 4q + 14 &= 0 \\\\ 18 + 12q + 4q + 14 &= 0 \\\\ 32 + 16q &= 0 \\\\ 16q &= -32 \\\\ q &= -2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nq=-2\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -6p + 4q + 14 &= 0 \\\\ -6p + 4\\left(-2\\right) + 14 &= 0 \\\\ -6p - 8 + 14 &= 0 \\\\ -6p + 6 &= 0 \\\\ -6p &= -6 \\\\ p &= 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $p=1$ and\n\n$$\nq=-2\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(1,-2\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505059 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $p - q$?\n\n$\\{2q + 16 = 0; 2p + 4q + 28 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{red}(1)$, we can solve for $q$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2q + 16 &= 0 \\\\ 2q &= -16 \\\\ q &= -8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $q=-8$ into equation $\\color{blue}(2)$ and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2p + 4q + 28 &= 0 \\\\ 2p + 4\\left(-8\\right) + 28 &= 0 \\\\ 2p - 32 + 28 &= 0 \\\\ 2p &= 4 \\\\ p &= 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p - q = 2 - \\left(-8\\right) = 10$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505062 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{3u - 10v - 24 = 0; 2u + 4v - 8 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{3u - 10v - 24 = 0(1); 2u + 4v - 8 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $u$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2u + 4v - 8 &= 0 \\\\ 2u + 4v &= 8 \\\\ 2u &= 8 - 4v \\\\ u &= \\dfrac{8 - 4v}{2} \\\\ u &= 4 - 2v \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $u=4-2v$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $v$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3u - 10v - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 3(4 - 2v) - 10v - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 12 - 6v - 10v - 24 &= 0 \\\\ -12 - 16v &= 0 \\\\ -16v &= 12 \\\\ v &= -\\dfrac{3}{4} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3u - 10v - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 3u - 10\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right) - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 3u + \\dfrac{15}{2} - 24 &= 0 \\\\ 3u - \\dfrac{33}{2} &= 0 \\\\ 3u &= \\dfrac{33}{2} \\\\ u &= \\dfrac{11}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $u=\\dfrac{11}{2}$ and\n\n$$\nv=-\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{11}{2},-\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505067 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-9x - 9y + 54 = 0; 4x + 8y - 16 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{-9x - 9y + 54 = 0(1); 4x + 8y - 16 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4x + 8y - 16 &= 0 \\\\ 4x + 8y &= 16 \\\\ 4x &= 16 - 8y \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{16 - 8y}{4} \\\\ x &= 4 - 2y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=4-2y$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -9x - 9y + 54 &= 0 \\\\ -9(4 - 2y) - 9y + 54 &= 0 \\\\ -36 + 18y - 9y + 54 &= 0 \\\\ 18 + 9y &= 0 \\\\ 9y &= -18 \\\\ y &= -2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\ny=-2\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -9x - 9y + 54 &= 0 \\\\ -9x - 9\\left(-2\\right) + 54 &= 0 \\\\ -9x + 18 + 54 &= 0 \\\\ -9x + 72 &= 0 \\\\ -9x &= -72 \\\\ x &= 8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=8$ and\n\n$$\ny=-2\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(8,-2\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505080 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{7x - 7y - 35 = 0; 2x - 4y - 6 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{7x - 7y - 35 = 0(1); 2x - 4y - 6 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2x - 4y - 6 &= 0 \\\\ 2x - 4y &= 6 \\\\ 2x &= 6 + 4y \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{6 + 4y}{2} \\\\ x &= 3 + 2y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=3+2y$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7x - 7y - 35 &= 0 \\\\ 7(3 + 2y) - 7y - 35 &= 0 \\\\ 21 + 14y - 7y - 35 &= 0 \\\\ -14 + 7y &= 0 \\\\ 7y &= 14 \\\\ y &= 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\ny=2\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 7x - 7y - 35 &= 0 \\\\ 7x - 7\\left(2\\right) - 35 &= 0 \\\\ 7x - 14 - 35 &= 0 \\\\ 7x - 49 &= 0 \\\\ 7x &= 49 \\\\ x &= 7 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=7$ and\n\n$$\ny=2\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(7,2\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505081 :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a - b$?\n\n$\\{4b + 32 = 0; 5a + 2b - 19 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{red}(1)$, we can solve for $b$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4b + 32 &= 0 \\\\ 4b &= -32 \\\\ b &= -8 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=-8$ into equation $\\color{blue}(2)$ and solve for $a$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 5a + 2b - 19 &= 0 \\\\ 5a + 2\\left(-8\\right) - 19 &= 0 \\\\ 5a - 16 - 19 &= 0 \\\\ 5a &= 35 \\\\ a &= 7 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a - b = 7 - \\left(-8\\right) = 15$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505084 :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-10x - 10y + 7 = 0; 4x + 8y - 16 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Sometimes, it's handy to assign a number to each equation in a system, so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\{-10x - 10y + 7 = 0(1); 4x + 8y - 16 = 0(2)\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $\\color{blue}(2)$, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 4x + 8y - 16 &= 0 \\\\ 4x + 8y &= 16 \\\\ 4x &= 16 - 8y \\\\ x &= \\dfrac{16 - 8y}{4} \\\\ x &= 4 - 2y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=4-2y$ into equation $\\color{red}(1)$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -10x - 10y + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -10(4 - 2y) - 10y + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -40 + 20y - 10y + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -33 + 10y &= 0 \\\\ 10y &= 33 \\\\ y &= \\dfrac{33}{10} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{33}{10}\n$$\n\ninto equation $\\color{red}(1)$, then we can find the value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -10x - 10y + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -10x - 10\\left(\\dfrac{33}{10}\\right) + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -10x - 33 + 7 &= 0 \\\\ -10x - 26 &= 0 \\\\ -10x &= 26 \\\\ x &= -\\dfrac{13}{5} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-\\dfrac{13}{5}$ and\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{33}{10}\n$$\n\n, which can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{13}{5},\\dfrac{33}{10}\\right)\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546991 :question/math-academy-id "q-18555" :question/problem "Given the system of equations below, what is the value of $a+b?$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2b &=6a+14 \\\\ 5b+25 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We number the equations so that we can refer to them more easily:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2b &=6a+14 && \\,\\,(1) \\\\ 5b+25 &=0. && \\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. Using equation $(2),$ we can solve for $b$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5b+25 & =0 \\\\ 5b & =-25 \\\\ b & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $b=-5$ into equation $(1)$ and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-5) & =6a+14 \\\\ -10 & =6a+14 \\\\ -10-14 & =6a \\\\ 6a & =-24 \\\\ a & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $a=-4,\\,b=-5.$ So,\n\n$$\na+b=-4+(-5)=-4-5=-9.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Challenging System of Linear Equations Using Substitution"}] :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations by Substitution"}
